pe":"rectangle","cornerRadius":10,"id":"WIxmc","x":1085,"y":360,"name":"Encoder focus","width":201,"height":300,"stroke":"#f2bf70","strokeWidth":3,"strokeAlignment":"inner"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"text","id":"MV9wz","x":120,"y":1532,"name":"c/ Implementation · Custom controls","fill":"#a3adbd","textGrowth":"fixed-width","width":1840,"content":"c/ Implementation · Custom controls (#763, #1026 part 4d, branch claude/custom-controls-mode-763)\nShipped: the mode itself and what a foot does in it. Every switch but MODE and BANK runs whatever the Pedals setup put on it; an unassigned one does nothing, since this is the one mode with no contextual defaults behind it. A switch with only a press acts on contact, one carrying both moves its press to the release. MODE stays the way out and BANK the way to the other four tracks, because the binding model refuses to hold an assignment on either. The track LEDs report the SWITCH rather than the track — lit when it carries an assignment — which is its own invariant: most of the catalogue has no on/off state a lamp could report, and what a performer needs before stomping is whether the switch does anything at all. Protocol v4 carries the mode, amber on the LED, degrading to mute below v4.\nNot shipped here: the Custom performance FACE on this canvas. The mode changes what the switches mean, and the screen still shows the Tracks face while it is in force; the tiles select and stop, because what a control does in Custom is assigned per footswitch and a tile is not one. The face is a separate piece of work.","lineHeight":1.4,"fontFamily":"Arimo","fontSize":28,"fontWeight":"normal"}]},{"type":"frame","id":"YLTza","x":6700,"y":20308,"name":"11  Performance · Transpose","width":6320,"height":4076,"fill":"#1a1d24","cornerRadius":24,"stroke":"#48505f","strokeWidth":2,"layout":"none","children":[{"type":"text","id":"EoSMp","x":120,"y":62,"name":"11  Performance · Transpose / Section title","fill":"#d5ddf5","content":"11  Performance · Transpose",